{"text": "Let's go closer. Much closer. The surface of the sensor looks like a mirror, but zoom in a few thousand times, and it turns into a city grid: tens of millions of identical cells, each about a micron across. A human hair would cover dozens of them. Each cell is a photodiode, a tiny well of silicon. When a particle of light, a photon, strikes the silicon, it knocks an electron loose, and the well holds on to it. More photons, more electrons. That's all a pixel is: a counter. But there's a catch. Silicon can count light, but it can't tell what colour it was. A red photon and a blue photon leave the same electron behind. So every well gets a filter on top, red, green, or blue, that only lets its own colour through. And above that, a tiny dome of a lens, that funnels light down into the well instead of letting it spill onto the wiring. Look at the pattern. In every block of four, there's one red, one blue, and two green. That isn't a mistake. Your eyes are most sensitive to green light, and it's where you pick up most of the brightness and detail, so the sensor spends half its pixels there. Here's the way to picture it. Imagine millions of buckets, set out in a rainstorm. Every photon is a raindrop. When the shutter closes, you measure how much water is in each bucket. In dim light, only a few drops fall, and it's down to luck which buckets they land in. One gets five, its neighbour gets two. That randomness is the grain you see in night photos. In bright light, you get the opposite problem. The bucket fills to the brim and overflows, and once it's full, it can't tell bright from blinding. That's a blown-out sky. When the exposure ends, each bucket is measured, and turned into a number. Millions of numbers. And that's what leaves the sensor.", "alignment": {"characters": ["L", "e", "t", "'", "s", " ", "g", "o", " ", "c", "l", "o", "s", "e", "r", ".", " ", "M", "u", "c", "h", " ", "c", "l", "o", "s", "e", "r", ".", " ", "T", "h", "e", " ", "s", "u", "r", "f", "a